WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.829
Regulation is the biggest driver of fund data today.

2
00:00:03.879 --> 00:00:12.081
In this film you see which regulatory data travels between fund houses, distributors and insurers, and how FundsXML carries it.

3
00:00:13.331 --> 00:00:17.828
Over the last decade, a series of European rules has changed the fund industry.

4
00:00:17.878 --> 00:00:23.124
MiFID two asks distributors to know the target market and the costs of every fund they sell.

5
00:00:23.174 --> 00:00:26.808
PRIIPs requires a key information document for retail investors.

6
00:00:26.858 --> 00:00:29.398
The SFDR asks for sustainability information.

7
00:00:29.448 --> 00:00:35.212
And Solvency two asks insurance companies to look through to the real holdings of the funds they own.

8
00:00:35.262 --> 00:00:40.565
Each of these rules needs data from the fund house, in large amounts, regularly, and on time.

9
00:00:40.615 --> 00:00:47.070
Without a common format, every fund house would have to deliver this data in a different layout to every partner.

10
00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:51.127
The industry answered with common templates.

11
00:00:51.177 --> 00:00:58.832
They are defined by FinDatEx, a joint body of the European associations of fund managers, insurers, banks and distributors.

12
00:00:58.882 --> 00:01:03.343
A template defines which fields are exchanged and what each field means.

13
00:01:03.393 --> 00:01:05.411
FundsXML carries these templates.

14
00:01:05.461 --> 00:01:15.372
The division of work is clear: FinDatEx decides what is exchanged, and FundsXML decides how it travels, together with all other fund data, in one checked file.

15
00:01:15.422 --> 00:01:24.644
FinDatEx publishes each template as a list of fields with their definitions; FundsXML turns each version into a structure that a computer can check.

16
00:01:25.994 --> 00:01:29.592
The European MiFID Template, EMT, is the most widely used.

17
00:01:29.642 --> 00:01:32.989
The fund house sends it to distributors and platforms.

18
00:01:33.039 --> 00:01:44.437
It describes the target market of each share class: which investors the fund is meant for, their knowledge and experience, their ability to bear losses, and their investment horizon.

19
00:01:44.487 --> 00:01:53.872
And it carries the costs: ongoing charges, transaction costs and entry and exit fees, so that the distributor can show investors what they really pay.

20
00:01:55.222 --> 00:01:57.066
How does a distributor use it?

21
00:01:57.116 --> 00:02:00.411
It receives EMT data for every share class it offers.

22
00:02:00.461 --> 00:02:05.776
Before a sale, it matches the client's profile against the target market of the fund.

23
00:02:05.826 --> 00:02:09.942
It shows the client the expected costs, before the client invests.

24
00:02:09.992 --> 00:02:13.919
And once a year, it reports the costs the client actually paid.

25
00:02:13.969 --> 00:02:20.925
Every step relies on data from the fund house, in a form that the distributor's systems can read automatically.

26
00:02:22.275 --> 00:02:27.432
The European PRIIPs Template, EPT, carries the data behind the key information document.

27
00:02:27.482 --> 00:02:30.805
The summary risk indicator, on a scale from one to seven.

28
00:02:30.855 --> 00:02:33.941
The performance scenarios, from stress to favourable.

29
00:02:33.991 --> 00:02:36.841
And the cost figures in the form PRIIPs requires.

30
00:02:36.891 --> 00:02:43.113
Whoever produces the key information document, the fund house or a service provider, works from this data.

31
00:02:43.163 --> 00:02:55.362
Because the key information document must be kept up to date, the data behind it travels again whenever a figure changes, and the receiver always knows from the file which version of the template it follows.

32
00:02:56.712 --> 00:03:00.479
The European ESG Template, EET, carries sustainability data.

33
00:03:00.529 --> 00:03:04.615
Is the fund an article six, eight or nine product under the SFDR?

34
00:03:04.665 --> 00:03:07.605
How much of it is aligned with the EU taxonomy?

35
00:03:07.655 --> 00:03:12.822
And how does it consider the principal adverse impacts on environment and society?

36
00:03:12.872 --> 00:03:18.611
The EET is by far the largest template, with several hundred fields, most of them optional.

37
00:03:18.661 --> 00:03:24.273
Distributors need it to match funds with the sustainability preferences of their clients.

38
00:03:24.323 --> 00:03:35.916
Data vendors often collect EET files from many fund houses and offer them to distributors in one place; because the structure is the same everywhere, this works without manual rework.

39
00:03:37.266 --> 00:03:40.816
The Tripartite Template, TPT, serves insurance companies.

40
00:03:40.866 --> 00:03:47.889
Under Solvency two, an insurer must calculate its capital requirement on the real holdings of the funds it owns.

41
00:03:47.939 --> 00:04:00.961
So the fund house delivers the full portfolio, position by position, with the data an insurer needs: a classification of each instrument, the so-called CIC code, its value, its issuer, and many risk figures.

42
00:04:01.011 --> 00:04:07.466
Insurers collect these files from every fund house, often each quarter, and load them all the same way.

43
00:04:08.816 --> 00:04:11.472
Most data flows from the fund house outwards.

44
00:04:11.522 --> 00:04:23.320
The European Feedback Template, EFT, goes the other way: distributors report back to the fund house how a fund was sold, for example to which types of clients and whether outside the target market.

45
00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:28.613
So the fund house can check whether its products reach the investors they were made for.

46
00:04:28.663 --> 00:04:32.642
It closes the loop between the two sides of the distribution chain.

47
00:04:33.992 --> 00:04:35.750
Here is the whole picture.

48
00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:42.634
The fund house sends EMT, EET and EPT data to distributors and platforms, and TPT data to insurers.

49
00:04:42.684 --> 00:04:46.668
Data vendors often collect and pass on the same templates.

50
00:04:46.718 --> 00:04:49.171
Distributors send EFT feedback back.

51
00:04:49.221 --> 00:04:58.211
And because all of it travels in FundsXML, one delivery can carry several templates together with the fund's prices and portfolio.

52
00:04:59.561 --> 00:05:02.576
What does this look like for a real share class?

53
00:05:02.626 --> 00:05:08.003
Here is the retail class of a fictional multi-asset fund, from the FundsXML examples.

54
00:05:08.053 --> 00:05:15.601
Its EMT data says the ongoing costs are one point five eight percent a year, and that the class suits retail investors.

55
00:05:15.651 --> 00:05:19.559
Its EET data says it is an article six product under the SFDR.

56
00:05:19.609 --> 00:05:24.348
And its TPT data lists forty-five positions for the insurer's look-through.

57
00:05:24.398 --> 00:05:28.434
Three templates, one share class, and all of it in one delivery.

58
00:05:29.784 --> 00:05:32.531
Templates change over time, as the rules change.

59
00:05:32.581 --> 00:05:40.805
Each template version gets its own place in the FundsXML schema, so an old version and a new one can be used side by side during a transition.

60
00:05:40.855 --> 00:05:45.933
Sender and receiver agree on the version they exchange, and move on when both are ready.

61
00:05:45.983 --> 00:05:52.867
And when a new version is published, it is built into the standard once, and not into every interface of every partner.

62
00:05:54.217 --> 00:05:56.598
Each template also has its own rhythm.

63
00:05:56.648 --> 00:06:02.035
EMT data is updated when costs or the target market change, and at least once a year.

64
00:06:02.085 --> 00:06:07.600
EET data follows changes in sustainability data, for example with the periodic reports.

65
00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:11.502
EPT data follows the updates of the key information document.

66
00:06:11.552 --> 00:06:16.812
And TPT data is needed for every reporting date of the insurer, often each quarter.

67
00:06:16.862 --> 00:06:21.929
Sender and receiver agree on these dates, so the data arrives when it is needed.

68
00:06:23.279 --> 00:06:27.701
Beyond the European templates, FundsXML also serves national reporting.

69
00:06:27.751 --> 00:06:37.715
Country modules carry data that only one country asks for, for example statistics for a national central bank, or tax data for investors in Austria or Germany.

70
00:06:37.765 --> 00:06:51.194
And as the European Single Access Point, ESAP, is being set up, fund documents and disclosures will become available in one place across the EU; structured data like FundsXML makes such reporting easier to produce.

71
00:06:52.544 --> 00:06:54.013
A few tips from practice.

72
00:06:54.063 --> 00:06:59.178
Know your receivers: which templates, which versions, and which dates each one needs.

73
00:06:59.228 --> 00:07:04.708
Fill the mandatory fields first, and then the optional ones that your receivers really use.

74
00:07:04.758 --> 00:07:09.994
Check every file before it leaves the house, against the schema and the business rules.

75
00:07:10.044 --> 00:07:17.529
And keep the history: send corrections as amend deliveries that refer to the original, instead of silently overwriting data.

76
00:07:18.879 --> 00:07:20.836
What does this mean in practice?

77
00:07:20.886 --> 00:07:27.860
A fund house produces its regulatory data once, in one format, and serves every distributor and insurer from it.

78
00:07:27.910 --> 00:07:31.372
Receivers load the data of all fund houses the same way.

79
00:07:31.422 --> 00:07:35.009
Every file can be checked automatically before it is sent.

80
00:07:35.059 --> 00:07:39.650
And when the next rule arrives, the industry adds it to the standard once.

81
00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:43.809
That was regulatory reporting with FundsXML.

82
00:07:43.859 --> 00:07:48.098
In the next film, you see how the quality of fund data is checked.

83
00:07:48.148 --> 00:07:49.398
Thanks for watching.
